            <ArticleTitle>Ureteral injury during laparoscopy-assisted anterior lumbar fusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1586-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A case report of ureteral injury as a complication incurred during a laparoscopy-assisted lumbar fusion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To alert orthopedic surgeons to this injury, which may occur during such surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Laparoscopy-assisted lumbar fusion is a minimally invasive surgical technique to accomplish lumbar fusion with excellent patient satisfaction, reduced hospital stay, and decreased rehabilitative time.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A case report is presented detailing ureteral injury as a complication of laparoscopy-assisted lumbar fusion and the subsequent treatment of the injury.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Laparoscopy-assisted lumbar fusion is a new, less invasive technique with excellent patient satisfaction; however, ureteral injury may occur, and the surgeon should keep this in mind if a postoperative fluid collection occurs in the pelvis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
